\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The longitudinal-field cost-reliability front for CrSBr at $\Delta = 20$. The bare path ($B_r = 0$) is half-hyperbolic; a field of order the anisotropy field removes the instability; the added cost grows as $B_r^2$. Success rates are over $400$ stochastic copies.}}{3}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:front}{{1}{3}{The longitudinal-field cost-reliability front for CrSBr at $\Delta = 20$. The bare path ($B_r = 0$) is half-hyperbolic; a field of order the anisotropy field removes the instability; the added cost grows as $B_r^2$. Success rates are over $400$ stochastic copies}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Success rate of the bare optimal pulse and of the same pulse with $B_r = 2\,K/\mu $, against the thermal stability factor, for CrSBr at ten Larmor times; 600 copies per point, 95 per cent binomial intervals. The added cost of the longitudinal field is $9.8\times 10^{-11}$~T$^2$~s at every point, 10.1 times the bare optimal cost. The single-site temperatures are sub-kelvin because one CrSBr site carries an anisotropy of 1.7~K in temperature units; device-grade stability comes from the exchange-coupled volume, not from one site.}}{4}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:delta}{{2}{4}{Success rate of the bare optimal pulse and of the same pulse with $B_r = 2\,K/\mu $, against the thermal stability factor, for CrSBr at ten Larmor times; 600 copies per point, 95 per cent binomial intervals. The added cost of the longitudinal field is $9.8\times 10^{-11}$~T$^2$~s at every point, 10.1 times the bare optimal cost. The single-site temperatures are sub-kelvin because one CrSBr site carries an anisotropy of 1.7~K in temperature units; device-grade stability comes from the exchange-coupled volume, not from one site}{table.2}{}}
